\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
The Wave Kinetic Equation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Yves Peysson, Lorenzo Morini and Joan Decker
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now derive the kinetic equation for the wave in presence of scattering
 by density and magnetic field fluctuations.
 From simple considerations about the incident wave and the fluctuations
 ordering we propose an appropriate parameter for the perturbative expansion
 of the Maxwell-Vlasov system of equations and we show that at the first
 and second order we obtain the Maxwell equations for the first and second
 order fields in closed form.
 By means of this perturbative analysis, we show the bilinear coupling between
 the incident field and the fluctuations field, that are treated as two
 independent linear waves, and we demonstrate that scattering in first Born
 approximation is a second order process due to this bilinear interaction.
 In the low temperature limit, the wave-wave interaction can be studied
 with an alternative fluid approach, based on the perturbative analysis
 of the MHD equations, that can be derived by a moments analysis of the
 Vlasov equations.
 We initially study the effects of the fluctuations on LH waves in the cold
 plasma limit, where all the temperature effects are neglected, so the fluid
 approach represents a good approximation.
 Also from the analysis of the MHD equations for a cold plasma it is easy
 to show that scattering is a second order effect.
 We derive an explicit expression for the second order current due to interactio
n between incident field and fluctuations in the cold plasma limit, starting
 from second order fluid equations.
 This expression is then inserted in the second order non homogeneous Maxwell
 equations, and the mode coupling equation that describes the coupling between
 the incident field and the fluctuations is derived in details.
 From this equation we can then derive the wave kinetic equation applying
 the random phase approximation, the wave kinetic equation describes the
 evolution of the wave energy density due to the scattering events in the
 plasma and its solution give us the evaluation of the scattering probability
 along the geometrical optics ray .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fluctuations ordering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now analyze the temporal and spatial scaling of the fluctuations comparing
 the fluctuations frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\omega}$
