\begin_layout Subsection
Time ordering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As we can observe from the values reported in the table, the frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f=\omega/2\pi$
\end_inset

 of the lower hybrid incident wave in tokamaks JET, TORE SUPRA and ALCATOR
 C-MODE is of the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $1GHz=10^{9}Hz$
\end_inset

, the LH system projected for ITER will works with 
\begin_inset Formula $f=5GHz$
\end_inset

, so of the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $10GHz=10^{10}Hz$
\end_inset

, a system that use this range of frequencies is just in operation on the
 FTU tokamak, in order to simulate the conditions of an LHCD discharge on
 ITER.
 In order to study the fluctuations effects in actual tokamaks scenarios,
 we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 of the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{9}Hz.$
\end_inset

 The fluctuations frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{f}=\widetilde{\omega}/2\pi$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{f}\approx100KHz=10^{5}Hz,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "VahBon"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zou"

\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\omega}\ll\omega$
\end_inset

 and the fluctuations time-scale is slower than incident wave time-scale,
 for the time-scales we have the small ordering parameter:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\delta^{2}=\frac{\widetilde{\omega}}{\omega}\approx\frac{10^{5}}{10^{9}}=10^{-4}\label{eq:timeordering}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to remember that this ordering parameter is obtained considering
 the characteristics of actual tokamaks scenarios, if we consider the parameters
 of an ITER-like scenario we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{2}=\widetilde{\omega}/\omega\approx10^{-5}.$
\end_inset

 We can then separate the temporal dynamics of the incident wave from the
 temporal dynamics of the fluctuations, the time derivative operator becomes
 (see appendix A):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t}=\frac{\partial}{\partial t_{\omega}}+\delta^{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial t_{\widetilde{\omega}}}\label{eq:timeop}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Space ordering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The perpendicular refractive index of the LH incident wave in the cold-electrost
atic approximation is given by the dispersion relation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
N_{\bot}^{2}=-\frac{K_{\Vert}^{C}}{K_{\bot}^{C}}N_{\Vert}^{2}\label{eq:NperpES}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where for a two species plasma the elements 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{\perp}^{C}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{\Vert}^{C}$
\end_inset

 of the cold plasma permittivity tensor, remembering that 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ci}^{2}\ll\omega^{2}\ll\omega_{ce}^{2}$
\end_inset

 are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
K_{\perp}^{C} & \simeq & 1+\frac{\omega_{pe}^{2}}{\omega_{ce}^{2}}-\frac{\omega_{pi}^{2}}{\omega^{2}}\\
K_{\Vert}^{C} & \simeq & 1-\frac{\omega_{pe}^{2}}{\omega^{2}}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our considerations, we assume the costant value 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\parallel}=2$
\end_inset

 that is the tipical initial value of the LH waves used in actual LHCD experimen
ts (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 in general remains of order 1 into the plasma).
 The values of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\perp}$
\end_inset

 and of the perpendicular component of the wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\perp}=\omega N_{\perp}/c$
\end_inset

 , in function of the normalized radial coordinate, calculated with the
 expression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
